{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for Draft2 exact267b59046fe1c588beb53dff8afa838d3c1f1861, sole primary artifact430e0890. Complete note and relevant current reservation/delivery/index/authority consumers read. This is design review, not Source approval.\n\nF7 P2 / Q10 — do not make compromise propagation permanently expendable while retaining existing publication behavior. Sections4.7/7/Q10 allow unrelated traffic to consume all root space outside C1, after which every compromised relay can remain pending at membership forever. The destination's compromised handler is the path that sets aborting, empties/revokes a live token and opens the deciding read (directory.ts344-362; destination.ts439-457,1729-1766). A later push attempt depends on aborting; destination's push rule does not make a fresh membership read (destination.ts1934-1964). Pending at the sender is consequently not equivalent to a pending harmless index update. Existing lanes can still have reserved/publishing/unresolved work after the root loses this capacity.\n\nDefine a bounded protection/admission protocol for required compromise relays, or a fail-closed publication alternative when that path cannot progress. An unbounded blanket reservation for every possible future notice is not required; the design must identify what is guaranteed and how the room avoids silently continuing the affected publication. Add a witness: fill unrelated root traffic to the new-work boundary with a held publication, revoke a key behind it, and verify the intended abort/revocation behavior across restart. Keep this as an explicit unresolved prerequisite if a separate contract/authority decision is needed; do not call the security behavior unchanged meanwhile.\n\nArchitecture — F1-F6 are otherwise answered at design level. The existing marker duty reserves the partition seal and its request closure while false (markers.ts; reserve.ts), so a late signer retains space. Keeping the repository control state open preserves the named selectors. Distinct terminal undelivered and pending delivery-unavailable cases now have explicit actions; C1 accounts separately for bounded generations and protects handoffs from unrelated traffic. Generation/number/seed indexes remove the old row-walk point reads, with C2 explicitly owed. No finite completion time is claimed. Directory10 avoids the membership directory9 collision. C1/C2 need separate adopted contracts before dependent Source; A1 is separate and not falsely claimed implemented.\n\nQ11 — confirmed only within the stated one-namespace resolver contract for a correctly addressed create: namespace.ts92-102 checks the seed digest/name, then accepts the seed route when the scope exists, including a refused genesis, and also accepts a create to an empty store. It returns neither not-found nor wrong-incarnation for that correct seed route. ScopeObject.deliver applies this before judgment; existing creations repeat through the recorded genesis. A misaddressed envelope can still get routing refusal even when some other child exists; an unavailable/unreachable service is not proof of nonexistence. State these preconditions rather than a universal transport claim. Complete attempt accounting and stable namespace identity remain necessary for the all-routing-refused conclusion.\n\nSecurity — F7 is the remaining blocker. Root/partition consumer affiliation and recorded-membership authority still need the promised Q6/Q7 source search and real authority witness; no new authority proof is inferred from kind directory alone. A1 remains subject to separate review of terminal-drop transport/version/admission semantics; its incomplete index includes never-created concern rows.\n\nSimplification — the stable flag, existing marker machinery, exact-key indexes and same-envelope operator resend are smaller than selector rewrites, row walks or a second seal protocol. Clarify that current operator.ts160-195 implements an explicit one-shot sendAgain; no automatic retry policy is currently designed after delivery-unavailable. Also change the ordinary compromised-relay budget example from always2 entries to allow its diagnosis plus result; it is capacity limited, not part of the handoff bound.\n\nVerification: static source/design comparison and diff whitespace check only. No code changes, tests/gate, pushes or deployments. Existing local Source approvals remain separate.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:430e0890978e27ab636adf377d115f647554c4eb","binding":"assigned","head":"267b59046fe1c588beb53dff8afa838d3c1f1861","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9c5c681996b086488749ba0f7aad771003e965f2\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:66bca0c253653385aa5325636d8de9481f07306a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fdc0a7e798ced04645ab1f716b631ff796c01703","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}